n't been started yet despite having package contracts in place.\n\nBut here's the critical constraint: the requirement_states field is what actually matters. It's the authoritative source, and it only marks srd110-pr and srd111-ptx as \"ready\" status. The rule is clear — I can only propose tasks for requirements that have \"ready\" status in requirement_states, which means I'm limited to just those two items regardless of what the code_status shows.\n\nNow I need to understand what these requirements actually entail. The project context gives me the requirement_states showing which R-items are ready, but it doesn't include the actual SRD content that would explain what pr and ptx need to do. And I can't read files or run commands to look them up, so I'm working with incomplete information here. I know pr is for paginating text files with headers, but I don't have the full requirement details for either tool. I'm realizing the weight constraints are key here—since I can't access the actual files, I need to work with what I know about the structure. The SRDs have been reorganized so weight-4 requirements are isolated, which means I can likely group the remaining requirements without exceeding the weight budget of 4 per task. For srd110-pr with six ready requirements, if they're mostly default weight-1, I should be able to combine them into one or two tasks depending on how the grouping works. The release notes mention that weight-4 requirements are in their own groups, which suggests some requirements might have that weight, but I don't have access to the actual requirements.yaml file to verify the weights. I'm working with the assumption that the pattern follows what's described, though I should note this limitation since the project context doesn't include the SRD contents or weight specifications. I'm going in circles trying to infer weights without the actual requirements file. Since I can only see which requirements are ready and the constraint mentions weight budgets, I'll just assume each sub-requirement has weight 1 and move forward with creating tasks that stay within the 4-requirement limit per task.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the task distribution: for the pr package, I can fit R3.1 through R4.3 into one task, leaving R5.1 and R5.2 for another. For ptx, I'm grouping R3.1 through R4.2 together and R5.1 with R5.2 separately. That gives me 4 tasks total, which respects the maximum. But I'm noticing the constraints also require a contract task before implementation tasks for new packages, and both pr and ptx are cmd/ packages, so I need to check what's already defined in the package contracts section.\n\nLooking at the code status, both packages show \"not started,\" meaning they don't exist yet. The contract requirement typically applies to shared pkg/ packages where other code depends on their exports, but cmd/ packages are self-contained binaries with just a main.go and test file. The existing contracts in the project are all for pkg/ packages, not cmd/ packages, which suggests cmd/ packages follow a simpler pattern and don't need formal contracts. I'm realizing that cmd/ packages don't export anything, so the contract requirement doesn't apply to them the same way it does for pkg/ packages—the constraint is really about shared library packages, not standalone binaries. Since the project's package_contracts section only lists pkg/ packages and there are no cmd/ contracts anywhere, I'll move forward without creating contracts for cmd/pr and cmd/ptx.\n\nNow I'm hitting a tension between two constraints: the planning constitution says to always split implementation and tests into separate tasks, but the 4-task limit means I can't do that without exceeding the cap. If I split each utility's implementation from its tests, I'd end up with 8 tasks total, which violates the hard limit. I need to either combine implementation and tests into single tasks, or focus on just one utility to stay within bounds. , P2 says \"Always split 