endency chain to ensure task 3 has access to both the test file from task 1 and the R5.1-R5.2 implementation from task 2, so I'll order them sequentially: implement the base features, then implement the advanced features, then test the base features, and finally test the advanced features with modifications to the existing test file.\n\nNow I'm working on the task descriptions themselves, making sure each one is self-contained with proper references to the SRD, the signal handler implementation in pkg/sys/, and the differential testing patterns in pkg/testutils/. The pr utility is essentially a text file paginator that mimics the behavior of the gpr reference binary from Homebrew coreutils, adding headers, page numbers, and multi-column layout.\n\nSince I can't directly read the SRD to understand what each requirement means, the best approach is to have the task descriptions reference the specific requirements by their identifiers—like R3.1 and R4.1—and instruct the stitch agent to read the SRD first to understand what needs implementing. That way the agent has the context it needs without me needing to interpret the requirements myself.\n\nLooking at the existing commit patterns, I can see tasks are structured to reference specific SRD requirement IDs, so I'll follow that same format. For the test tasks, I'll use the existing test pattern as a template—building the binary and comparing it against the reference implementation.\n\nI need to be careful not to invent design decisions beyond what's in the SRD or architecture, so I'll stick to what I know like using sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() and keep the rest grounded in the actual requirements.\n\nLooking at the roadmap, the use case for this work is marked as spec_complete, so I should reference the use case file at docs/specs/use-cases/rel15.0-uc003-pr.yaml and the test suite at docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-15.0.yaml in the required reading.\n\nI'm also reconsidering the task distribution—the constraint limits me