d the test suite specifications. I'm thinking through which design decisions actually derive from the requirements versus which are just implementation details.\n\nThe key design decisions I can extract are: calling sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main, using gpr from Homebrew coreutils as the reference binary for testing, applying the pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests pattern for differential testing, and skipping tests if the reference binary isn't available. I should also note that pr is a GNU coreutils pagination utility that formats text with headers, supports multi-column output, and has various formatting flags.\n\nSince I can't access the actual SRD, my requirement descriptions will be generic and point to the specification document for the stitch agent to read. One more thing to verify: the constraint that each task must be independently executable means I need to ensure the task descriptions don't create hidden dependencies.\n\nWriting the YAML now...\n\nActually, I need to reconsider the contract task requirement. The constraint says to propose a contract task for new packages, but cmd/pr is a main package, not a library package, so it won't export types or interfaces. The contract concept only applies to packages in the pkg/ directory based on the project structure.\n\nI'll skip the contract task for cmd/ packages and move forward with writing the final output—the project state summary, priority analysis, weight budget check, and YAML task list. Let me also verify there are no existing implementations I'm missing.\n\nLooking at the code status, both rel15.0-uc003-pr and rel15.0-uc004-ptx show \"not started,\" which is what I expected. The requirement states show R3.1 through R5.2 are ready for both SRDs, but I should double-check whether the earlier requirements like R1.x and R2.x are actually complete or just not listed in the ready section.\n\nThe constraint is clear though—I should only propose tasks for items explicitly marked as \"ready,\" so the unlisted requirements must fall into a different status category. I'm confident in this interpretation now, so I'll start drafting the YAML output with the ready requirements.\n\nGenerating task YAML... I'm laying out the remaining requirements for the pr utility implementation, along with the key design decisions around SIGPIPE handling and module structure, plus the acceptance criteria that need to be met for compilation and linting. I need to modify the existing main.go file to add R5.1 and R5.2 functionality while keeping the prior implementation intact, ensuring it compiles and meets the acceptance criteria outlined in the SRD. Now I'm looking at the test files and utilities I'll need to reference—the DiffTest struct and existing test patterns—to write differential tests that verify the pr implementation matches the gpr reference binary for the R3.1 and R4.1 requirements. I'm setting the locale environment variable to C for consistent output comparison across different systems, and using temporary directories for test input files. The acceptance criteria cover compilation, test execution with graceful skipping if the reference binary isn't available, and comprehensive coverage of all the requirements from the specification.\n\nNow I need to review the SRD document, existing test suite, and the current implementation to understand what differential tests are needed for the R5.1 and R5.2 requirements, then add those tests to the existing test file using the DiffTest utilities. Finishing up the acceptance criteria—the test cases need to cover both R5.1 and R5.2 from the specification, and I want to make sure the existing tests for the earlier requirements still pass. Let me verify the checklist one more time: four tasks total, each with manageable sub-requirements, proper dependency ordering from implementation through testing, everything tied to release 15.0, and the YAML structure matches what we need. The design decisions are grounded in the actual requireme